\chapter{Failure, fallback, and recovery}
MuLL distinguishes a failed library lookup from an ordinary evaluation error.
\api{G:LoadLib} returns nil on failure and stores a diagnostic. Arithmetic
errors, invalid calls, failed assertions, and explicit \api{DieWith} calls
throw an error to the embedding application.

\begin{mullcode}
val absent <- G:LoadLib("not_installed");
G:Assert(absent == nil);
val detail <- "$E?";
G:Assert(detail == "library not found: not_installed");
val choice <- absent or {enabled: false};
G:Assert(choice:enabled == false);
\end{mullcode}

\section{Fallback is not exception handling}
\api{or} inspects the truth of its left operand. It does not catch arbitrary
exceptions. For example, division by zero on the left of \api{or} still
terminates the run. The import idiom works because \api{LoadLib} deliberately
converts discovery, parsing, and package-evaluation failures into nil.

Use \api{G:DieWith(message)} when a fallback cannot make progress. It raises
the supplied display text. \api{G:Assert(value)} returns its input when true
and raises ``assertion failed'' otherwise.

\section{Host recovery}
Catch \api{mull::Error} in C++ or \api{MuLL.Error} in D. Both expose the source
line and column in addition to the message. A failed run does not roll back
earlier native calls or assignments. The host can run another source string
on the same instance, inspect retained bindings, or discard the instance and
start a fresh session.
